<!--
  /control-flow — {#if}, {#for}, {#match}. The three control-flow
  directives. They are partials; they splice a branch into the surrounding
  template without a wrapper element.
-->
<div class="doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">Concepts</p>
  <h1>Control flow</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    SUI's template directives are <em>partials</em> — they splice a branch into
    the surrounding template without creating an extra wrapper element. The
    compiler turns each into a small re-render callback scoped to its parent.
  </p>

  <h2>&#123;#if&#125; · conditional render</h2>
  <p>Render a branch when a signal is truthy. The <code>&#123;:else&#125;</code> branch is
    optional. The whole block toggles in place — no diffing of unrelated parts
    of the tree.</p>
  <h2>&#123;#for&#125; · loop render</h2>
  <p>Iterate over an array signal. The <code>key</code> attribute tells the
    runtime how to identity items between renders; supply a unique primitive
    (often the item id) so reorders and removes don't churn the DOM.</p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.for}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>&#123;#match&#125; · multi-way branch</h2>
  <p>Match on a discriminated union — an enum, a tagged result. Each
    <code>&#123;:case&#125;</code> arms a single variant. The compiler turns the match
    into a single switch and only the matching branch mounts.</p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.match}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>Why partials</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>div</code> wrapper around every directive would be wasteful and
    awkward — most of these branches are inline siblings. By splicing at
    compile time, SUI keeps the runtime DOM aligned with the authored DOM:
    every branch appears in the tree exactly the way you wrote it.
  </p>

  <div class="page-nav">
    <a class="btn" href="/components-controllers">← Components &amp; controllers</a>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/data-binding">Data binding →</a>
  </div>
</div>
